text:
We investigate the $K	o \pi 
u\overline{
u}$ decay mode in the Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (MSSM) framework, with a focus on the implications of the $\epsilon^{\prime}_K/\epsilon_K$ anomaly. Our analysis seeks to reconcile the theoretical predictions with the observed experimental data, thereby shedding light on the underlying physics. We employ a combination of theoretical tools, including the renormalization group equations and the effective field theory approach, to study the decay process within the MSSM. Our results indicate that the $\epsilon^{\prime}_K/\epsilon_K$ anomaly significantly impacts the $K	o \pi 
u\overline{
u}$ branching ratio, leading to a substantial increase in its predicted value. We demonstrate that this enhancement is a consequence of the interplay between the supersymmetric partners of the $D$ and $F$ quarks, which contribute to the $\epsilon^{\prime}_K/\epsilon_K$ ratio. Our findings suggest that the MSSM can accommodate the observed anomaly while preserving the experimental constraints on the $K	o \pi 
u\overline{
u}$ decay mode. We also explore the potential implications of this result for the search for supersymmetry in high-energy colliders.
